<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uzbekistan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uzbekistan is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> covering much of historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, including the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, whose capital was Samarkand, and an important part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a>. Many of the region's great medieval monuments date from the Timurid period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uzbekistan.jpg" alt="Uzbekistan" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Farhod Yoʻldoshev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uzbekistan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Uzbekistan has an area of 448,978 square kilometres and stretches 1,425 kilometres from west to east. It is doubly landlocked: none of its rivers reach the sea, since they lie in a series of endorheic basins. It is the only Central Asian state that borders all four of the others. Less than 10% of its territory is intensively cultivated irrigated land in river valleys and oases, and the rest is the vast Kyzylkum Desert and mountains. The climate is continental, with 100 to 200 millimetres of precipitation a year. The highest point is Khazret Sultan at 4,643 metres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a> has been largely desiccated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> production established in the Soviet era.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During early antiquity the region was inhabited by nomadic Scythian tribes, who built an extensive irrigation system along the rivers when they settled, and cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> emerged as centres of government and high culture. By the fifth century BCE the Bactrian, Sogdian and Tokharian states dominated the region. Sogdian intermediaries became the wealthiest intermediaries in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and the West, which made Bukhara and Samarkand extremely wealthy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and Chorasmia as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>, and in 327 BCE Alexander the Great conquered Sogdiana and Bactria, meeting fierce popular resistance. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a> in the first century BCE.</p><p>In the eighth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, which soon became a focal point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a>. The Mongol invasion of the 13th century destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, and in the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> from here. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> became a centre of science under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a>. Turko-Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a> conquered the Timurid lands in the 16th century, and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> was gradually incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the 19th, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> the political centre of Russian Turkestan. The Uzbek Soviet Socialist Republic was created in 1924, and independence was declared in 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Uzbekistan is a secular state with a semi-presidential government, made up of 12 regions, Tashkent City and the autonomous republic of Karakalpakstan. Uzbek is the official language and Russian serves as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>, while Tajik is spoken mainly in the south around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. Uzbeks form more than 89% of the population, and most people are Muslim. Many of the great monuments of the Timurid period survive in Samarkand. Uzbekistan is a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uzbekistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city of Qarshi in <b>Uzbekistan</b> takes its name from the palace that Kebek Khan built there, near Nasaf, in the early 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Their stronghold was Tokharistan, on the northern slopes of the Hindu Kush in today's southern <b>Uzbekistan</b> and northern Afghanistan, with a capital probably at Kunduz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He rose from the minor nobility of the Barlas tribe in Transoxiana (modern <b>Uzbekistan</b>), within the Chagatai Khanate, through military talent, political ruthlessness and skilful use of the Mongol framework of legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their early coinage imitated Greco-Bactrian issues, and at Khalchayan in southern <b>Uzbekistan</b> a palace or hall was decorated with painted clay sculptures of nomad aristocrats in Hellenistic style.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 20th century Turkey adopted the Latin alphabet in 1928, the Turkic peoples of the Soviet Union were made to write in Cyrillic, and after 1991 several independent states, such as Azerbaijan, <b>Uzbekistan</b> and Turkmenistan, moved to Latin alphabets, as Kazakhstan has begun to do.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Today it is the official language of Iran, an official language of Afghanistan, as Dari, and of Tajikistan, as Tajik, and is spoken by large communities in <b>Uzbekistan</b> and by a worldwide diaspora.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
